<Project>

  <!--
    Target frameworks.

    net10.0 only, deliberately. The net11.0 target was dropped in #16: it existed to pick up
    System.IO.Compression.ZstandardStream from the BCL, but the .NET 11 preview SDK is not
    installed on dev machines, so that branch was compiled nowhere and its risk was structural
    rather than incidental. Restore it when .NET 11 is GA — see Internal/ZstdDecompressor.cs,
    which is still the one seam that would need to change.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LibraryTargetFrameworks>net10.0</LibraryTargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Native AOT / trimming friendliness is a hard requirement for the codec, but the gate for it
    lives in Directory.Build.targets, not here. It depends on $(IsTestProject), which the test
    project sets in its own body; Directory.Build.props is imported before that body is
    evaluated, so a Condition here would always see an empty value. See Directory.Build.targets.
  -->

  <!--
    The NodaTime convention (CLAUDE.md, "Dates and times") enforced by the compiler rather than
    by memory. BannedApiAnalyzers reports every use of a banned BCL date/time type as RS0030,
    which TreatWarningsAsErrors above turns into a build error carrying the NodaTime replacement
    in its message.

    Applied to every project, tests included: a test that reaches for DateTime to build an
    expected value is exactly where the 100 ns truncation would be laundered into a passing
    assertion. Analyzer-only, PrivateAssets="all", so nothing reaches consumers.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    0.10.0 — the fifth package reaches the feed (#102).

    DatabentoDotNet.Extensions.Hosting has existed in this repository since M6 and on nuget.org
    never. This is its first published version, and publishing it is the whole point of the
    release: the core four spent 0.9.0 and 0.9.1 installable and unpromised, which is what produced
    the one piece of evidence 1.0.0 was waiting for, and the fifth package had been given no
    equivalent.

    ROADMAP section 8 had reserved it for 1.1.0 and that is superseded rather than contradicted.
    Its objection was to a *promise* — five packages locked to one SemVer undertaking on the day the
    fifth appeared — and 0.x carries no promise at all. What section 8 could not anticipate is that
    the next release would not be 1.0.0, so "after 1.0" and "with an evidence window" stopped being
    the same instruction. Under the 1.1.0 plan the window was the interval when the package was not
    installable, which is a worse window than the core four were given rather than an equal one.

    0.10.0 and not 0.9.2, though everything here is additive and the policy would allow a patch.
    0.9.1's own release commit fixed what a patch means in this repository: no non-comment line
    changed anywhere under src. This carries a new package id and about 3,300 new source lines, and
    a consumer reading 0.9.1 to 0.9.2 would have no signal that a fifth package now exists. The
    version string is the only thing many of them read.

    The core four are almost unchanged: HistoricalClient gained Handler and DisposesHandler (#86),
    four entries, additive. Dbn, Live and Reference have no public surface change at all.

    Every PublicAPI.Shipped.txt is still empty, all five of them, and that is unchanged rather than
    overlooked. Shipped records a surface undertaken not to break; 0.x undertakes nothing. #68 is
    still where that moves, and it now has one fewer special case to make.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>0.10.0</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Herbert Sabanal</Authors>
    <Product>databento-dotnet</Product>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/jerbersoft/databentodotnet</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <!--
      NuGet renders this as "Project website", and from 0.9.1 it is the documentation site rather
      than the repository (#85). That reverses a decision recorded on #68, so here is why.

      #68 chose the repository URL on two grounds. First, projectUrl is baked per version and is
      permanent, and a repository URL survives a rename or an owner transfer because GitHub redirects
      both. Second — quoting it — "the wiki is the landing page this would otherwise have needed".

      The second ground is gone: #82 retired the wiki and built the site. The first still has force,
      and honestly so, because jerbersoft.github.io/databentodotnet is weaker than the repository URL
      — renaming the repository breaks the Pages URL without redirecting it.

      It is reversed anyway, because that risk is already taken in the same artefact. Each of the four
      package READMEs hardcodes several jerbersoft.github.io guide URLs, and those freeze into the
      package page exactly as this property does. Pointing projectUrl at the site adds no new class of
      permanence risk; it only stops "Project website" going somewhere less useful than the README
      rendered directly beneath it.

      What #68 rejected was a *custom domain* behind a Cloudflare Worker, which carries a registration
      that can lapse independently of the project. A github.io URL is tied to the same account as the
      repository and fails only when the repository does. RepositoryUrl above is unchanged and is
      still what SourceLink uses; NuGet renders it separately as "Source repository", so nothing is
      lost from the page.
    -->
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://jerbersoft.github.io/databentodotnet/</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageTags>databento;market-data;dbn;trading;real-time;historical</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      A pointer, not a copy (#74). nuspec metadata is frozen at pack time and cannot be corrected
      afterwards, so release notes baked into a package go stale in the one place nobody can edit
      them. CLAUDE.md allows exactly one canonical copy of each fact; a URL is how a package
      participates in that without becoming a second copy. It is also version-independent, which is
      why it can sit here rather than being maintained per release.

      This pointed at the wiki until #82 moved the release notes onto the documentation site. The
      0.9.0 packages already on nuget.org still carry the old wiki URL and always will — nuspec
      metadata is frozen, which is the exact hazard the paragraph above opens by describing, now
      demonstrated on itself.

      That link is degraded rather than dead: with the wiki disabled, GitHub 302s every /wiki/*
      path to the repository home page, so a 0.9.0 reader lands somewhere real and one click from
      the notes rather than on a 404. Verified after the deletion, not assumed — the outcome was
      better than the prediction, and the prediction is what this comment said before it was
      checked.

      0.9.1 is the correction (#85), and it is the only kind available: a frozen package page cannot
      be edited, only superseded by a version whose page is right. Every version from here carries
      the URL below.
    -->
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Release notes: https://jerbersoft.github.io/databentodotnet/release-notes.html</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
